A 56-year-old male with a 30-year history of heavy alcohol abuse presents with acute disorientation, confusion, asterixis, and progressive ascites. He is diagnosed with decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis and portal hypertension. Physical examination reveals massive ascites, 3+ bilateral lower extremity pitting edema, and jaundice, confirming expanded total extracellular fluid (ECF) volume. Despite total body fluid overload, serum chemistry reveals severe dilutional hypoosmolar hyponatremia: serum sodium = 120 mEq/L, potassium = 3.4 mEq/L, chloride = 88 mEq/L, bicarbonate = 22 mEq/L, BUN = 8 mg/dL, and plasma osmolarity = 252 mOsm/kg. Spot urinalysis reveals a concentrated urine osmolarity of 520 mOsm/kg with a spot urine sodium of 8 mEq/L. Timed urine collection demonstrates a urine flow rate (V_dot) of 0.60 mL/min, yielding an osmolar clearance (Cosm) of 1.24 mL/min and a free-water clearance (CH2O) of -0.64 mL/min.

Trace the complete 5-step pathophysiology of this condition through the following sequential cascade:
1. The primary hepatic vascular pathology (splanchnic arterial vasodilation) and the resulting drop in effective arterial blood volume (EABV) despite expanded total ECF volume;
2. The sensory perception by arterial baroreceptors that triggers persistent non-osmotic secretion of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and activation of the RAAS;
3. The cellular signaling cascade in collecting duct principal cells (V2-Gs-AC-cAMP-PKA) driving apical AQP2 trafficking and medullary osmotic water extraction;
4. The biophysical free-water clearance calculation proving negative free-water clearance (CH2O < 0) and failure to dilute the urine; and
5. The fundamental physiological dilemma explaining why administering isotonic 0.9% normal saline or fluid boluses fails to correct the hyponatremia and instead accelerates peripheral edema and ascites formation.

